KNOWNBUG
out_of_range_index_write1.sv
--bound 1
^\[main\.p0\] always 1: PROVED up to bound 1$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A write to an unpacked array element with a constant index outside the
declared range is rejected with a hard error "array index out of range"
/ CONVERSION ERROR (exit code 2), thrown in
verilog_rtl_buildert::decompose_lhs (src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp, the
ID_verilog_bit_select case; both the unpacked and packed array branches
have this check).  Per IEEE 1800-2017 7.4.6, writing to an array with an
invalid index shall perform no operation, so the assignment must be a
silent no-op (at most a warning) and the property must be PROVED with
EXIT=0.  For bit/part selects on vectors the same file already follows
11.5.1 by clipping out-of-range slices in assign_to instead of erroring;
array element writes should be handled the same way.
